  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008132: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8132 Photographic Products Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the
`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing
ADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an
autonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a
`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a
robotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/
administrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that
gates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates
high-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8132
-- Photographic Products Machine Operators** only, Wave3
(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3
batches 1-12 already landed: 72 occupations so far, the entire 75xx
craft-trades range and initial 81xx mining/plant range complete).

The `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8132\" ...}` entry
was verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M
-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println
(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"8132\\\"))\"` from a checkout with
the `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling present at the correct
`../technology` relative path), and its `:name` read exactly
\"Photographic Products Machine Operators\" -- confirming the premise,
no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec`
with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and
no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8132` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before
scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the
`8132` entry to `:implemented`.

Photographic Products Machine Operators run film/photo-processing
equipment using darkroom chemicals (developer, fixer, and historically
some toxic silver-based/chromium compounds) -- a chemical-exposure
hazard, plus an equipment hazard from rollers and processing
machinery. The controlling constraint for this vertical is that the
closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly finalizes
a processing-operation-execution decision or a chemical-safety-
clearance decision, nor override a plant safety officer's judgment --
those decisions must always be a hard permanent block, never
auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to PLANT
SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never operates processing
equipment or makes chemical-safety-clearance decisions itself.

The reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the
closed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is
[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8112`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8112)
(Mineral and Stone Processing Plant Operators: `store`/`advisor`/
`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,
an already-landed closely-related plant-operation safety pattern) --
cloned and read in full in this session, independently re-tested
(fresh clone, `clojure -M:test` re-run green: 23 tests / 49
assertions, 0 failures / 0 errors, confirming its \"VERIFIED working\"
status before mirroring).

## Decision

Publish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8132` as an OSS plant
scheduling/logistics-coordination actor for photographic products
machine operators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as
every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Photographic
Products Machine Operations Coordination Advisor ⊣
PhotoLabCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph
(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the
reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the
`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),
with an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via
`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.

This actor is deliberately **plant scheduling/logistics coordination
ONLY, never direct processing-operation-execution or chemical-safety-
clearance authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise,
simulate exercising, or propose exercising a processing-operation-
execution decision, a chemical-safety-clearance decision, or any
override of a plant safety officer's judgment. These are not merely
gated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the
op-allowlist entirely.

### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)

- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data
  logging
- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal
- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a chemical-exposure/equipment-
  condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit eligible
- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- darkroom-chemical/film-stock
  procurement proposal

This actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a
processing-operation-execution decision or a chemical-safety-clearance
decision, nor an override of a plant safety officer's judgment. These
are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely
gated behind escalation -- an op such as
`:finalize-processing-operation-decision` cannot even be proposed by
the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only
ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a
forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever
produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check
(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,
independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names seven
concretely-forbidden ops (`photolab.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:
`:finalize-processing-operation-decision`,
`:authorize-processing-operation`,
`:proceed-with-processing-operation`,
`:declare-chemical-safety-cleared`,
`:finalize-chemical-safety-clearance`,
`:override-plant-safety-officer-judgment`,
`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is
verified by dedicated unit tests
(`photolab.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-processing-operation-decision`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-processing-operation`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-processing-operation`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-chemical-safety-cleared`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-plant-safety-officer-judgment`).

### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)

1. **Operator unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an
   independently registered photo-processing equipment operator
   record (`:no-operator`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of
   who the operator is.
2. **Plant unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently
   registered plant site (`:no-plant`). Together with check 1, this
   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that an operator/plant
   record must be independently verified/registered before any
   action.
3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD
   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)
   (`:no-actuation`).
4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the
   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),
   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the seven
   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`
   names a finalization/execution action for a processing-operation-
   execution decision, a chemical-safety-clearance decision, or an
   override of a plant safety officer's judgment, is a second HARD,
   PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated
   unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of the
   allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the
   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks
   processing-operation-execution/chemical-safety-clearance/safety-
   officer-override language wherever it appears in free text.

### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor

- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of
  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the
  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a chemical-exposure/
  equipment-condition observation -- it never resolves the concern
  itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard data,\"
  never \"here is what I recommend doing about plant safety.\"
- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds
  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by
  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human
  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a
  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold
  darkroom-chemical/film-stock order is not itself unsafe, unlike a
  processing-operation-execution or chemical-safety-clearance/safety-
  officer-override attempt, which is why it is handled by a different
  mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural absence).
- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for
  any op.)

### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)

A governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.
\"developer\", \"fixer\", \"silver\") can accidentally match inside the mock
advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,
self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk
this vertical's own vocabulary (a plant coordination actor working
adjacent to darkroom-chemical processing equipment necessarily
discusses those concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could
trigger if phrased carelessly. `photolab.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`
is phrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g.
\"finalize the processing-operation decision\", \"declare the chemical
safety cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\" --
matching the task's own worked example phrasing), never bare nouns,
and
`photolab.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`
is a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed
allowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips
`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale
template was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged
work record for operator ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for
developer/fixer processing-line shift ...\", \"... routed for plant
safety officer review\") WITHOUT containing any of the full
finalization-action phrases, so the regression test is a genuine
stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a
vacuous pass. Green on first run in this session -- the
scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during
development.
`photolab.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`
additionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,
confirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`
regardless of disposition path.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8132\" ...}` entry
  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`
  set to the real repository
  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8132` /
  `cloud-itonami-isco-8132`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented
  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via
  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately
  before the edit).
- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 23 tests, 49 assertions, 0
  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's
  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified
  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just
  the local working copy).
- `clj-kondo` lint (`clojure -M:lint` -- this actor's `deps.edn`
  defines a `:lint` alias, matching the `cloud-itonami-isco-8131`
  sibling's more current `deps.edn` pattern rather than the slightly
  older `cloud-itonami-isco-8112` reference, which lacks one): 0
  errors, 0 warnings in the final pushed commit (one pre-existing
  unused-binding warning in `photolab.advisor`'s `infer` was found and
  fixed during this session by dropping an unused `:as request`
  destructure binding -- a trivial no-behavior-change cleanup, tests
  re-confirmed green after the fix, before push).
- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via
  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with
  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.
- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's
  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry
  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.
- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention
  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to
  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string
  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.

## Alternatives considered

- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-processing-operation` or
  `:approve-chemical-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an
  always-escalate list: rejected outright per the task's explicit
  domain-design guidance and prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` precedent
  (e.g. `cloud-itonami-isco-8112`) -- an always-escalate op still
  means the *capability* to finalize a processing-operation-execution
  decision or a chemical-safety-clearance decision exists in the code
  path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this
  severe (chemical-exposure hazard, equipment hazard from
  rollers/processing machinery), the capability itself must not exist.
  The only op related to plant-safety escalation is
  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for human/plant-
  safety-officer attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or
  defers a processing-operation or chemical-safety-clearance decision
  for later automatic execution.
- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block: rejected per the task's
  explicit domain design -- an over-threshold darkroom-chemical/
  film-stock order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft
  escalate gate, not a hard block; only the processing-operation-
  execution/chemical-safety-clearance/safety-officer-override class is
  a hard, unconditional block.
- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"developer\" \"fixer\"
  \"silver\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug
  class described above, and per CLAUDE.md's own worked example for
  this exact task, which explicitly warns against exactly this pattern
  -- phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS
  (\"finalize the processing operation\", \"declare the plant safety
  cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"), never
  bare nouns (\"developer\", \"fixer\", \"silver\"). This actor's own domain
  necessarily discusses developer/fixer/silver concepts to do its
  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking
  the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly
  against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).
- Collapsing the processing-operation-execution exclusion and the
  chemical-safety-clearance exclusion into a single generic
  \"operational-decision\" exclusion category: rejected -- the task's
  domain design names these as two textually distinct forbidden
  decision classes (\"finalize the processing-operation decision\" vs.
  \"declare the chemical safety cleared\"), so this ADR keeps them as
  separate concretely-named ops and separate scope-excluded phrase
  entries (with dedicated tests for each) rather than merging them,
  matching the task's explicit worked phrasing examples verbatim.
- Merging `operator` and `plant` into a single entity (only checking
  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape
  (independently-registered operator AND independently-registered
  plant) matches the CRITICAL invariant that an \"operator/plant record
  must be independently verified/registered before any action\" and
  the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors
  with two-sided provenance checks.

## Itemized safety confirmation

Every op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is
safe under the constraints above:

1. **`:log-work-record`** -- production-run/inventory/progress data
   logging only. Requires an independently registered operator AND
   plant (`:no-operator`/`:no-plant`). Not a processing-operation-
   execution or chemical-safety-clearance decision in any respect --
   it is a records-management action.
2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/shift/task scheduling
   proposal only. Cannot carry a processing-operation-execution
   decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such
   attempt via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action`
   content check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any
   conclusion about a specific processing operation -- it is a
   calendar/logistics action.
3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a chemical-exposure/
   equipment-condition concern to a human/plant safety officer; ALWAYS
   escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER
   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a
   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never
   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself, nor does it ever
   declare a chemical safety clearance -- the plant safety officer
   reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.
4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- darkroom-chemical/film-stock
   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not a processing-operation-
   execution or chemical-safety-clearance decision of any kind -- it
   is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling
   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).

No op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles
directly finalizing a processing-operation-execution decision or a
chemical-safety-clearance decision, nor overriding a plant safety
officer's judgment -- these categories are structurally absent, not
gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself
(`photolab.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) seven concretely-named
forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level
(`photolab.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,
`photolab.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-processing-operation-decision`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-processing-operation`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-processing-operation`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-chemical-safety-cleared`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-plant-safety-officer-judgment`),
and (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`
field (`photolab.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,
`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale
that attempts to finalize a processing-operation decision, declare the
chemical safety cleared, or override a plant safety officer's judgment
even via an otherwise-allowlisted op
(`photolab.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-plant-safety-officer`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-chemical-safety-cleared`).")))
